{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RfmeAgriwDd9gFoELJ4aZY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test rationale — SOS 97, Ral Zarek, Guest Lecturer\n2\t\n3\tLegendary Planeswalker — Ral, `{1}{B}{B}`, starting loyalty 3. Four loyalty\n4\tabilities:\n5\t\n6\t- **+1: Surveil 2.**\n7\t- **−1: Any number of target players each discard a card.**\n8\t- **−2: Return target creature card with mana value 3 or less from your\n9\t  graveyard to the battlefield.**\n10\t- **−7: Flip five coins. Target opponent skips their next X turns, where X is\n11\t  the number of coins that came up heads.**\n12\t\n13\t## Contract assumptions (drawn from existing engine + FDN planeswalkers)\n14\t\n15\tThe engine has a `Planeswalker` base class (`engine/card.py`) with\n16\t`starting_loyalty`/`loyalty` and `get_loyalty_abilities()`, plus a\n17\t`LoyaltyAbility(loyalty_cost, effect, description)` dataclass. The completed\n18\tFDN planeswalkers (`cards/fdn/fdn_44` Kaito, `cards/fdn/fdn_234` Vivien,\n19\t`cards/fdn/fdn_176` Liliana) establish the convention these tests assume:\n20\t\n21\t- `get_loyalty_abilities()` returns a `list[LoyaltyAbility]`; each `effect` is\n22\t  a closure `(game) -> None` that reads `pw.controller`.\n23\t- Targeted loyalty abilities read a card-set attribute for their chosen\n24\t  target(s). FDN uses `pw._resolve_target` (single object); these tests extend\n25\t  that to `pw._resolve_targets` (a list) for the multi-target −1 ability, and\n26\t  keep `pw._resolve_target` for the single-target −2 and −7 abilities.\n27\t- \"You may\" / per-card decisions use the `DeterministicPlayer` choice channels\n28\t  (`choose_yes_no`, `choose_card`), matching `cards/fdn/fdn_234`.\n29\t\n30\tEach ability is exercised in isolation by calling `ability.effect(game)` after\n31\tsetting `pw.controller` and the relevant target/decision inputs — the same\n32\tisolation style as the FDN reference tests, which call `effect` directly rather\n33\tthan driving the whole loyalty-activation pipeline.\n34\t\n35\t## `cards/sos/sos_97/tests.py`\n36\t\n37\t### TestRalZarekProperties (8 tests)\n38\tStatic identity per the spec: is a `Planeswalker`, has `CardType.PLANESWALKER`,\n39\tname, `{1}{B}{B}` mana cost, `LEGENDARY` supertype, `Ral` subtype, and\n40\t`starting_loyalty`/`loyalty` both initialised to 3. These pin the printed\n41\tcharacteristics down so the implementation can't drift on type line or loyalty.\n42\t\n43\t### TestRalZarekLoyaltyAbilityShape (3 tests)\n44\t- `get_loyalty_abilities()` returns exactly four abilities.\n45\t- Each is a `LoyaltyAbility` instance.\n46\t- The four `loyalty_cost` values are exactly `{+1, −1, −2, −7}` — validates the\n47\t  printed loyalty costs and that no ability is missing or duplicated.\n48\t\n49\t### TestRalZarekSurveil (4 tests) — the +1\n50\tSurveil 2 (CR 701.25) looks at the top two library cards and puts any number of\n51\tthem into the graveyard, the rest back on top. Decisions are scripted through\n52\tthe controller's `choose_yes_no`.\n53\t- Declining both leaves the library size and graveyard unchanged.\n54\t- Binning both moves exactly those two top cards to the graveyard and leaves the\n55\t  card beneath them in the library (verifies it operates on the *top two* only).\n56\t- Surveil never puts a card into hand (it is not a draw/dig-to-hand effect).\n57\t- Surveil with an empty library is a safe no-op (CR 701.25a/c — looking at fewer\n58\t  than 2 cards is fine and surveil 0 just does nothing).\n59\t\n60\t### TestRalZarekDiscard (4 tests) — the −1\n61\t- A single targeted opponent discards exactly one card, which ends in their\n62\t  graveyard.\n63\t- Two targeted players each discard one card.\n64\t- Zero targets is legal (\"any number of target players\") and nobody discards.\n65\t- A targeted player with an empty hand is handled without raising and produces\n66\t  no graveyard card.\n67\t\n68\t### TestRalZarekReanimate (5 tests) — the −2\n69\t- Returns a mana-value-3 creature card from the controller's graveyard to the\n70\t  battlefield (and removes it from the graveyard). Mana value is read from\n71\t  `mana_cost.cmc`.\n72\t- Mana value 0 is within \"3 or less\" and is returned.\n73\t- Mana value 4 is *not* returned (boundary of the \"3 or less\" filter).\n74\t- A non-creature card (a cheap sorcery) is not returned (only creature cards are\n75\t  legal).\n76\t- No chosen target is a safe no-op.\n77\t\n78\t### TestRalZarekUltimate (3 tests) — the −7\n79\t- The ultimate exists at loyalty cost −7.\n80\t- It runs without raising when a target opponent is supplied (five coin flips +\n81\t  skip scheduling execute).\n82\t- With no target it is a safe no-op and does not corrupt game state.\n83\t\n84\tThe exact value of X (number of heads across five flips) and the *enforcement*\n85\tof skipping the opponent's next X turns are not asserted — see the edge cases\n86\tbelow and `untestable.json`.\n87\t\n88\t## Edge cases deliberately NOT covered (and why)\n89\t\n90\t- **Exact coin-flip result / X computation (−7).** The engine has no coin-flip\n91\t  primitive, and the implementer's RNG entry point is unknown (could be\n92\t  `random.random`, `random.randint`, `random.choice`, …). Asserting a specific\n93\t  X would over-couple the test to an unspecified internal. Recorded in\n94\t  `untestable.json`.\n95\t- **Enforced turn-skipping (−7).** \"Skip your next X turns\" is a replacement\n96\t  effect (CR 614.10) with no native engine support — only an `extra_turns`\n97\t  FIFO exists, and the spec docstring on `GameState` explicitly flags skip-turn\n98\t  as not fully handled. There is no observable, engine-defined surface to assert\n99\t  that the opponent's next X turns are skipped. Recorded in `untestable.json`.\n100\t- **Loyalty-cost payment / once-per-turn / sorcery-speed gating.** These are the\n101\t  engine's `activate_ability` responsibilities (`engine/abilities.py`,\n102\t  exercised by `engine_tests`), not this card's contract. Out of scope here.\n103\t- **`Surveil` as a static keyword.** The spec lists \"Surveil\" under keywords,\n104\t  but Surveil is an action keyword used inside the +1 text, not an evergreen\n105\t  member of `engine.types.Keyword`. The card therefore should not advertise a\n106\t  `Keyword.SURVEIL` flag, so no such membership test is written; surveil is\n107\t  validated behaviourally instead.\n108\t\n109\t## Risks / soft assumptions\n110\t\n111\t- The surveil decision channel is assumed to be per-card `choose_yes_no`. If the\n112\t  implementation instead asks for a single subset via `choose`/`choose_card`,\n113\t  the two scripted surveil tests would need re-scripting. The structural\n114\t  invariants (no cards to hand; empty-library no-op; operates on top two only)\n115\t  are robust regardless.\n116\t- The target-passing attributes are assumed to be `pw._resolve_target`\n117\t  (single) and `pw._resolve_targets` (list), extending the FDN `_resolve_target`\n118\t  convention. If the implementer chooses a different attribute name, the\n119\t  targeted-ability tests become a legitimate test/impl dispute.\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c2fbc8b-9529-4254-888d-bbf9dd1c7145","uuid":"93bfa61c-9343-40c6-a0dd-f73aeb35d700","timestamp":"2026-06-02T00:45:48.068Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_97/test-rationale.md","content":"# Test rationale — SOS 97, Ral Zarek, Guest Lecturer\n\nLegendary Planeswalker — Ral, `{1}{B}{B}`, starting loyalty 3. Four loyalty\nabilities:\n\n- **+1: Surveil 2.**\n- **−1: Any number of target players each discard a card.**\n- **−2: Return target creature card with mana value 3 or less from your\n  graveyard to the battlefield.**\n- **−7: Flip five coins. Target opponent skips their next X turns, where X is\n  the number of coins that came up heads.**\n\n## Contract assumptions (drawn from existing engine + FDN planeswalkers)\n\nThe engine has a `Planeswalker` base class (`engine/card.py`) with\n`starting_loyalty`/`loyalty` and `get_loyalty_abilities()`, plus a\n`LoyaltyAbility(loyalty_cost, effect, description)` dataclass. The completed\nFDN planeswalkers (`cards/fdn/fdn_44` Kaito, `cards/fdn/fdn_234` Vivien,\n`cards/fdn/fdn_176` Liliana) establish the convention these tests assume:\n\n- `get_loyalty_abilities()` returns a `list[LoyaltyAbility]`; each `effect` is\n  a closure `(game) -> None` that reads `pw.controller`.\n- Targeted loyalty abilities read a card-set attribute for their chosen\n  target(s). FDN uses `pw._resolve_target` (single object); these tests extend\n  that to `pw._resolve_targets` (a list) for the multi-target −1 ability, and\n  keep `pw._resolve_target` for the single-target −2 and −7 abilities.\n- \"You may\" / per-card decisions use the `DeterministicPlayer` choice channels\n  (`choose_yes_no`, `choose_card`), matching `cards/fdn/fdn_234`.\n\nEach ability is exercised in isolation by calling `ability.effect(game)` after\nsetting `pw.controller` and the relevant target/decision inputs — the same\nisolation style as the FDN reference tests, which call `effect` directly rather\nthan driving the whole loyalty-activation pipeline.\n\n## `cards/sos/sos_97/tests.py`\n\n### TestRalZarekProperties (8 tests)\nStatic identity per the spec: is a `Planeswalker`, has `CardType.PLANESWALKER`,\nname, `{1}{B}{B}` mana cost, `LEGENDARY` supertype, `Ral` subtype, and\n`starting_loyalty`/`loyalty` both initialised to 3. These pin the printed\ncharacteristics down so the implementation can't drift on type line or loyalty.\n\n### TestRalZarekLoyaltyAbilityShape (3 tests)\n- `get_loyalty_abilities()` returns exactly four abilities.\n- Each is a `LoyaltyAbility` instance.\n- The four `loyalty_cost` values are exactly `{+1, −1, −2, −7}` — validates the\n  printed loyalty costs and that no ability is missing or duplicated.\n\n### TestRalZarekSurveil (4 tests) — the +1\nSurveil 2 (CR 701.25) looks at the top two library cards and puts any number of\nthem into the graveyard, the rest back on top. Decisions are scripted through\nthe controller's `choose_yes_no`.\n- Declining both leaves the library size and graveyard unchanged.\n- Binning both moves exactly those two top cards to the graveyard and leaves the\n  card beneath them in the library (verifies it operates on the *top two* only).\n- Surveil never puts a card into hand (it is not a draw/dig-to-hand effect).\n- Surveil with an empty library is a safe no-op (CR 701.25a/c — looking at fewer\n  than 2 cards is fine and surveil 0 just does nothing).\n\n### TestRalZarekDiscard (4 tests) — the −1\n- A single targeted opponent discards exactly one card, which ends in their\n  graveyard.\n- Two targeted players each discard one card.\n- Zero targets is legal (\"any number of target players\") and nobody discards.\n- A targeted player with an empty hand is handled without raising and produces\n  no graveyard card.\n\n### TestRalZarekReanimate (5 tests) — the −2\n- Returns a mana-value-3 creature card from the controller's graveyard to the\n  battlefield (and removes it from the graveyard). Mana value is read from\n  `mana_cost.cmc`.\n- Mana value 0 is within \"3 or less\" and is returned.\n- Mana value 4 is *not* returned (boundary of the \"3 or less\" filter).\n- A non-creature card (a cheap sorcery) is not returned (only creature cards are\n  legal).\n- No chosen target is a safe no-op.\n\n### TestRalZarekUltimate (3 tests) — the −7\n- The ultimate exists at loyalty cost −7.\n- It runs without raising when a target opponent is supplied (five coin flips +\n  skip scheduling execute).\n- With no target it is a safe no-op and does not corrupt game state.\n\nThe exact value of X (number of heads across five flips) and the *enforcement*\nof skipping the opponent's next X turns are not asserted — see the edge cases\nbelow and `untestable.json`.\n\n## Edge cases deliberately NOT covered (and why)\n\n- **Exact coin-flip result / X computation (−7).** The engine has no coin-flip\n  primitive, and the implementer's RNG entry point is unknown (could be\n  `random.random`, `random.randint`, `random.choice`, …). Asserting a specific\n  X would over-couple the test to an unspecified internal. Recorded in\n  `untestable.json`.\n- **Enforced turn-skipping (−7).** \"Skip your next X turns\" is a replacement\n  effect (CR 614.10) with no native engine support — only an `extra_turns`\n  FIFO exists, and the spec docstring on `GameState` explicitly flags skip-turn\n  as not fully handled. There is no observable, engine-defined surface to assert\n  that the opponent's next X turns are skipped. Recorded in `untestable.json`.\n- **Loyalty-cost payment / once-per-turn / sorcery-speed gating.** These are the\n  engine's `activate_ability` responsibilities (`engine/abilities.py`,\n  exercised by `engine_tests`), not this card's contract. Out of scope here.\n- **`Surveil` as a static keyword.** The spec lists \"Surveil\" under keywords,\n  but Surveil is an action keyword used inside the +1 text, not an evergreen\n  member of `engine.types.Keyword`. The card therefore should not advertise a\n  `Keyword.SURVEIL` flag, so no such membership test is written; surveil is\n  validated behaviourally instead.\n\n## Risks / soft assumptions\n\n- The surveil decision channel is assumed to be per-card `choose_yes_no`. If the\n  implementation instead asks for a single subset via `choose`/`choose_card`,\n  the two scripted surveil tests would need re-scripting. The structural\n  invariants (no cards to hand; empty-library no-op; operates on top two only)\n  are robust regardless.\n- The target-passing attributes are assumed to be `pw._resolve_target`\n  (single) and `pw._resolve_targets` (list), extending the FDN `_resolve_target`\n  convention. If the implementer chooses a different attribute name, the\n  targeted-ability tests become a legitimate test/impl dispute.\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":120}}}
