{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QULugqg46BDpnXpkhHSETA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test rationale — SOS 201, Lorehold, the Historian\n2\t\n3\tCard: `{3}{R}{W}` Legendary Creature — Elder Dragon, 5/5, colors R/W.\n4\t\n5\tOracle text:\n6\t1. Flying, haste\n7\t2. Each instant and sorcery card in your hand has miracle {2}.\n8\t3. At the beginning of each opponent's upkeep, you may discard a card. If you\n9\t   do, draw a card.\n10\t\n11\tTest file: `cards/sos/sos_201/tests.py`\n12\t\n13\t## TestLoreholdProperties — static characteristics\n14\t- `test_is_creature` — must subclass `Creature`.\n15\t- `test_name` / `test_mana_cost` / `test_power_toughness` — printed values from\n16\t  the spec (`{3}{R}{W}`, 5/5).\n17\t- `test_is_legendary` — `Supertype.LEGENDARY` (the type line is \"Legendary\n18\t  Creature\").\n19\t- `test_elder_dragon_subtypes` — `{\"Elder\", \"Dragon\"}` (subset check, so an\n20\t  implementation that adds more subtypes still passes).\n21\t- `test_colors_are_red_white` — colors are observable via the `self.colors`\n22\t  convention established in KEY_DECISIONS (sos_13). The card is R/W.\n23\t\n24\t## TestLoreholdKeywords — flying + haste and their combat consequences\n25\t- `test_has_flying_and_haste` — both `Keyword.FLYING` and `Keyword.HASTE` flags\n26\t  set.\n27\t- `test_haste_allows_attacking_while_summoning_sick` — drives the engine's real\n28\t  `_can_attack`; a summoning-sick Lorehold can still attack because of haste.\n29\t- `test_ground_creature_cannot_block_flying_lorehold` — flying restricts legal\n30\t  blockers; a vanilla ground creature cannot block (engine `_can_block`).\n31\t- `test_flying_creature_can_block_flying_lorehold` /\n32\t  `test_reach_creature_can_block_flying_lorehold` — fliers and reach creatures\n33\t  *can* block a flier; these confirm legal blockers are not wrongly excluded.\n34\t  (These two pass against the bare stub because it has no flying yet; they remain\n35\t  correct after implementation and guard against an over-broad block restriction.)\n36\t\n37\t## TestLoreholdMiracleGrant — \"Each instant and sorcery card in your hand has miracle {2}\"\n38\tThe engine has **no native miracle / alternative-cost / draw-reveal pipeline**\n39\t(confirmed: `grep -i miracle` matches only the spec and the rulebook; no engine\n40\tsupport). Miracle is a granted *static* ability (CR 702.94a). Following the\n41\tproject convention for unsupported keywords (e.g. Paradigm in sos_120, which is\n42\tadvertised via a card marker), these tests target the observable, spec-faithful\n43\tportion of the contract: the card exposes the granted cost and stamps it onto\n44\tthe eligible cards in *its controller's* hand.\n45\t\n46\t- `test_miracle_cost_is_two_generic` — the granted alternative cost is `{2}`\n47\t  (asserted via a `MIRACLE_COST` constant on the card).\n48\t- `test_grants_miracle_to_instant_in_controller_hand` /\n49\t  `test_grants_miracle_to_sorcery_in_controller_hand` — a `grant_miracle(game)`\n50\t  method marks instants and sorceries in the controller's hand with\n51\t  `miracle_cost == {2}`.\n52\t- `test_does_not_grant_miracle_to_noncast_types_in_hand` — creatures and lands\n53\t  in hand are not instants/sorceries and must not receive a miracle cost.\n54\t- `test_does_not_grant_miracle_to_opponent_hand` — \"your hand\" is the\n55\t  controller's hand; the opponent's instants/sorceries are unaffected.\n56\t\n57\tAssumed contract (documented so the Coordinator/Implementer can align): a card\n58\tmethod `grant_miracle(game)` and a `MIRACLE_COST` class/instance constant. The\n59\tdeeper timing rule — \"you may cast it for {2} when you draw it if it's the first\n60\tcard you drew this turn\" — depends on a draw→reveal→cast-for-alternative-cost\n61\tpipeline that the engine does not have, so that portion is recorded in\n62\t`untestable.json`.\n63\t\n64\t## TestLoreholdUpkeepTriggerRegistration / Condition / Effect — the opponent-upkeep rummage\n65\t`BeginningOfUpkeepTriggeredEvent` is fired by `engine/turn.py` with **no player\n66\targument**, so the trigger's condition must read `game.active_player` to tell\n67\twhose upkeep it is. The ability fires only on an *opponent's* upkeep.\n68\t\n69\t- `test_registers_an_upkeep_trigger` — `register_triggers` adds exactly one\n70\t  `BeginningOfUpkeepTriggeredEvent` trigger sourced from the card and controlled\n71\t  by its controller.\n72\t- `test_does_not_fire_on_controllers_own_upkeep` — with the controller as active\n73\t  player, firing the event pushes nothing (edge case: must not loot on your own\n74\t  upkeep). (Passes against the bare stub since no trigger exists yet; becomes a\n75\t  real negative assertion once the trigger is implemented.)\n76\t- `test_fires_on_opponents_upkeep` — with the opponent active, the trigger\n77\t  pushes a StackObject sourced from the card, controlled by the card's\n78\t  controller.\n79\t- `test_yes_discards_chosen_card_and_draws` — \"you may discard a card. If you do,\n80\t  draw a card\": yes path discards the chosen card to the graveyard and draws the\n81\t  top of library into hand.\n82\t- `test_no_does_not_discard_or_draw` — declining leaves the hand intact and draws\n83\t  nothing.\n84\t- `test_empty_hand_is_safe_noop` — with an empty hand the controller cannot\n85\t  discard, so the conditional draw does not happen and the effect must not raise.\n86\t  Robust to either implementation order (check-empty-first, or ask-then-find-no-card).\n87\t\n88\t## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered\n89\t- The full miracle *cast-when-drawn* timing (reveal as you draw, first-card-this-\n90\t  turn restriction, casting for the {2} cost). No engine surface exists for it;\n91\t  see `untestable.json`.\n92\t- Multiple-opponent upkeep ordering. The engine is a fixed two-player game\n93\t  (`non_active_player` assumes two players), so \"each opponent\" reduces to the\n94\t  single opponent — already covered by the opponent-upkeep tests.\n95\t- Legend-rule / state-based actions for the legendary supertype — that is engine\n96\t  behavior covered by `engine_tests/`, not this card's contract.\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c2fbc8b-9529-4254-888d-bbf9dd1c7145","uuid":"d52cabfc-cc06-4ec4-8c25-3799b546bc09","timestamp":"2026-06-02T01:18:12.560Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_201/test-rationale.md","content":"# Test rationale — SOS 201, Lorehold, the Historian\n\nCard: `{3}{R}{W}` Legendary Creature — Elder Dragon, 5/5, colors R/W.\n\nOracle text:\n1. Flying, haste\n2. Each instant and sorcery card in your hand has miracle {2}.\n3. At the beginning of each opponent's upkeep, you may discard a card. If you\n   do, draw a card.\n\nTest file: `cards/sos/sos_201/tests.py`\n\n## TestLoreholdProperties — static characteristics\n- `test_is_creature` — must subclass `Creature`.\n- `test_name` / `test_mana_cost` / `test_power_toughness` — printed values from\n  the spec (`{3}{R}{W}`, 5/5).\n- `test_is_legendary` — `Supertype.LEGENDARY` (the type line is \"Legendary\n  Creature\").\n- `test_elder_dragon_subtypes` — `{\"Elder\", \"Dragon\"}` (subset check, so an\n  implementation that adds more subtypes still passes).\n- `test_colors_are_red_white` — colors are observable via the `self.colors`\n  convention established in KEY_DECISIONS (sos_13). The card is R/W.\n\n## TestLoreholdKeywords — flying + haste and their combat consequences\n- `test_has_flying_and_haste` — both `Keyword.FLYING` and `Keyword.HASTE` flags\n  set.\n- `test_haste_allows_attacking_while_summoning_sick` — drives the engine's real\n  `_can_attack`; a summoning-sick Lorehold can still attack because of haste.\n- `test_ground_creature_cannot_block_flying_lorehold` — flying restricts legal\n  blockers; a vanilla ground creature cannot block (engine `_can_block`).\n- `test_flying_creature_can_block_flying_lorehold` /\n  `test_reach_creature_can_block_flying_lorehold` — fliers and reach creatures\n  *can* block a flier; these confirm legal blockers are not wrongly excluded.\n  (These two pass against the bare stub because it has no flying yet; they remain\n  correct after implementation and guard against an over-broad block restriction.)\n\n## TestLoreholdMiracleGrant — \"Each instant and sorcery card in your hand has miracle {2}\"\nThe engine has **no native miracle / alternative-cost / draw-reveal pipeline**\n(confirmed: `grep -i miracle` matches only the spec and the rulebook; no engine\nsupport). Miracle is a granted *static* ability (CR 702.94a). Following the\nproject convention for unsupported keywords (e.g. Paradigm in sos_120, which is\nadvertised via a card marker), these tests target the observable, spec-faithful\nportion of the contract: the card exposes the granted cost and stamps it onto\nthe eligible cards in *its controller's* hand.\n\n- `test_miracle_cost_is_two_generic` — the granted alternative cost is `{2}`\n  (asserted via a `MIRACLE_COST` constant on the card).\n- `test_grants_miracle_to_instant_in_controller_hand` /\n  `test_grants_miracle_to_sorcery_in_controller_hand` — a `grant_miracle(game)`\n  method marks instants and sorceries in the controller's hand with\n  `miracle_cost == {2}`.\n- `test_does_not_grant_miracle_to_noncast_types_in_hand` — creatures and lands\n  in hand are not instants/sorceries and must not receive a miracle cost.\n- `test_does_not_grant_miracle_to_opponent_hand` — \"your hand\" is the\n  controller's hand; the opponent's instants/sorceries are unaffected.\n\nAssumed contract (documented so the Coordinator/Implementer can align): a card\nmethod `grant_miracle(game)` and a `MIRACLE_COST` class/instance constant. The\ndeeper timing rule — \"you may cast it for {2} when you draw it if it's the first\ncard you drew this turn\" — depends on a draw→reveal→cast-for-alternative-cost\npipeline that the engine does not have, so that portion is recorded in\n`untestable.json`.\n\n## TestLoreholdUpkeepTriggerRegistration / Condition / Effect — the opponent-upkeep rummage\n`BeginningOfUpkeepTriggeredEvent` is fired by `engine/turn.py` with **no player\nargument**, so the trigger's condition must read `game.active_player` to tell\nwhose upkeep it is. The ability fires only on an *opponent's* upkeep.\n\n- `test_registers_an_upkeep_trigger` — `register_triggers` adds exactly one\n  `BeginningOfUpkeepTriggeredEvent` trigger sourced from the card and controlled\n  by its controller.\n- `test_does_not_fire_on_controllers_own_upkeep` — with the controller as active\n  player, firing the event pushes nothing (edge case: must not loot on your own\n  upkeep). (Passes against the bare stub since no trigger exists yet; becomes a\n  real negative assertion once the trigger is implemented.)\n- `test_fires_on_opponents_upkeep` — with the opponent active, the trigger\n  pushes a StackObject sourced from the card, controlled by the card's\n  controller.\n- `test_yes_discards_chosen_card_and_draws` — \"you may discard a card. If you do,\n  draw a card\": yes path discards the chosen card to the graveyard and draws the\n  top of library into hand.\n- `test_no_does_not_discard_or_draw` — declining leaves the hand intact and draws\n  nothing.\n- `test_empty_hand_is_safe_noop` — with an empty hand the controller cannot\n  discard, so the conditional draw does not happen and the effect must not raise.\n  Robust to either implementation order (check-empty-first, or ask-then-find-no-card).\n\n## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered\n- The full miracle *cast-when-drawn* timing (reveal as you draw, first-card-this-\n  turn restriction, casting for the {2} cost). No engine surface exists for it;\n  see `untestable.json`.\n- Multiple-opponent upkeep ordering. The engine is a fixed two-player game\n  (`non_active_player` assumes two players), so \"each opponent\" reduces to the\n  single opponent — already covered by the opponent-upkeep tests.\n- Legend-rule / state-based actions for the legendary supertype — that is engine\n  behavior covered by `engine_tests/`, not this card's contract.\n","numLines":97,"startLine":1,"totalLines":97}}}
