{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gfcg79XnH4VrTqJ828pp56","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test rationale — sos_226 Silverquill, the Disputant\n2\t\n3\tCard: `{2}{W}{B}` Legendary Creature — Elder Dragon, 4/4.\n4\tOracle: \"Flying, vigilance. Each instant and sorcery spell you cast has\n5\tcasualty 1. (As you cast that spell, you may sacrifice a creature with power 1\n6\tor greater. When you do, copy the spell and you may choose new targets for the\n7\tcopy.)\"\n8\t\n9\tTest file: `/workspace/cards/sos/sos_226/tests.py` (27 tests).\n10\t\n11\t## Assumed API contract (so the Implementer can align)\n12\t\n13\tThe engine has **no native casualty mechanic**, and `engine.casting.cast_spell`\n14\tdoes **not** fire any `SpellCastTriggeredEvent`. Following the established\n15\tproject convention for granted abilities lacking native engine support\n16\t(SOS 201's miracle granting via `grants_miracle_to` / `grant_miracle`, and the\n17\t`copy_spell` surface used by FDN 248), the tests assume the card exposes:\n18\t\n19\t- `SilverquillTheDisputant` subclasses `engine.card.Creature` with:\n20\t  - `name == \"Silverquill, the Disputant\"`, `mana_cost == {2}{W}{B}`,\n21\t    `base_power == 4`, `base_toughness == 4`,\n22\t    `supertypes` ⊇ `{LEGENDARY}`, `subtypes` ⊇ `{\"Elder\", \"Dragon\"}`,\n23\t    `keywords` ⊇ `FLYING | VIGILANCE`, and `self.colors == [\"W\", \"B\"]`\n24\t    (KEY_DECISIONS sos_13 explicit-colors convention).\n25\t- `CASUALTY_AMOUNT` class constant equal to `1` (the granted casualty value).\n26\t- `grants_casualty_to(spell) -> int | None` — returns `1` for an\n27\t  instant/sorcery, `None` otherwise (mirrors `grants_miracle_to`).\n28\t- `offer_casualty(game, stack_obj) -> bool` — the casualty offer. Given a spell\n29\t  already on the stack as a `StackObject` (with `is_spell=True`), it:\n30\t  1. returns `False` (no-op, no prompt) if the spell is not controlled by the\n31\t     dragon's controller, or is not an instant/sorcery, or the controller has\n32\t     no creature with `power >= 1` to sacrifice;\n33\t  2. otherwise asks `choose_yes_no` to pay casualty; on decline returns `False`;\n34\t  3. on accept asks `choose_card` to pick a power>=1 creature to sacrifice\n35\t     (the legal options must exclude power-0 creatures), sacrifices it\n36\t     (battlefield → graveyard), then asks `choose_yes_no` whether to choose new\n37\t     targets for the copy; if yes, `choose_target` provides the new target;\n38\t  4. pushes a `copy_spell(...)` of the spell onto the stack (a distinct object\n39\t     from the original, `is_spell=True`, controlled by the dragon's controller,\n40\t     with either the new targets or the original targets) and returns `True`.\n41\t\n42\tDecision order consumed from the player script in the accept path:\n43\t`choose_yes_no (pay)` → `choose_card (sacrifice)` → `choose_yes_no (new targets?)`\n44\t→ `choose_target (new target, only if yes)`. The Implementer may reorder the\n45\t\"new targets\" question relative to the copy, but the tests script exactly one\n46\tyes/no for it after the sacrifice. This contract was validated end-to-end with a\n47\tthrowaway reference implementation: all 27 tests pass against it, then fail\n48\tagainst the stub (TDD red phase).\n49\t\n50\t## Test groups\n51\t\n52\t### TestSilverquillProperties (7)\n53\tStatic characteristics: is a `Creature`, name, mana cost `{2}{W}{B}`, 4/4,\n54\tLEGENDARY supertype, Elder Dragon subtypes, and `self.colors == {W, B}`.\n55\t\n56\t### TestSilverquillKeywords (6)\n57\t- `test_has_flying_and_vigilance` — both keyword flags present.\n58\t- Flying combat consequences through the real `_can_block`: a ground creature\n59\t  cannot block; a flyer or a reach creature can. The flyer/reach tests guard on\n60\t  `Keyword.FLYING in attacker.keywords` so they only pass when Silverquill truly\n61\t  flies (otherwise they would pass trivially against a non-flying stub).\n62\t- `test_vigilance_does_not_tap_when_attacking` — drives the real\n63\t  `declare_attackers_step`; the dragon is declared as an attacker and must\n64\t  remain untapped (`is_attacking is True`, `is_tapped is False`), exercising the\n65\t  engine's vigilance handling.\n66\t\n67\t### TestSilverquillCasualtyGrant (5)\n68\tThe granted casualty value and eligibility capability: `CASUALTY_AMOUNT == 1`;\n69\t`grants_casualty_to` returns `1` for an instant and a sorcery, and `None` for a\n70\tcreature spell and a land.\n71\t\n72\t### TestSilverquillCasualtyOffer (9)\n73\tThe core behavior, driving `offer_casualty` against a hand-built spell\n74\t`StackObject`:\n75\t- accept → sacrifice a power>=1 creature and push exactly one distinct copy of\n76\t  the spell;\n77\t- decline → no sacrifice, no copy;\n78\t- no power>=1 creature available → no copy, **and no prompt** (verified via the\n79\t  empty player script: a stray prompt raises `ScriptExhaustedError`);\n80\t- power-0 creature is not a legal sacrifice option (a `choose_card` spy captures\n81\t  the offered options and asserts the 0/4 wall is excluded while the 1/1 is\n82\t  included);\n83\t- choosing new targets points the copy at a different target than the original;\n84\t- declining new targets keeps the copy's original targets;\n85\t- the pushed copy is `is_spell=True` and controlled by the dragon's controller;\n86\t- an opponent-controlled spell on the stack gets no offer (no prompt, no copy) —\n87\t  \"spells *you* cast\";\n88\t- a creature spell on the stack gets no offer — casualty is instant/sorcery only.\n89\t\n90\t### TestSilverquillCastingIntegration (1)\n91\t`test_offer_casualty_on_really_cast_spell` — casts an instant through the REAL\n92\t`engine.casting.cast_spell`, locates the genuine spell `StackObject` the engine\n93\tpushed, then drives `offer_casualty` against it and asserts the sacrifice +\n94\tsingle copy. This proves the offer composes with a real cast (not just a\n95\thand-built StackObject).\n96\t\n97\t## Edge cases covered and why\n98\t- Eligibility filter (power >= 1) — exercised both as a no-op (no eligible\n99\t  creature) and as an option-filtering check (power-0 excluded).\n100\t- \"You cast\" ownership gating (opponent's spell ignored).\n101\t- Card-type gating (creature spell ignored; instant and sorcery both granted).\n102\t- New-targets opt-in vs. opt-out for the copy.\n103\t- Vigilance observed through real combat (no tap), not just the flag.\n104\t\n105\t## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered (out of scope / unblockable)\n106\t- **Automatic wiring of the casualty offer into `cast_spell`.** The engine\n107\t  fires no spell-cast event and the granted spell's own `on_cast` is not\n108\t  Silverquill's, so there is no guaranteed automatic hook. The integration test\n109\t  therefore drives `offer_casualty` explicitly against a really-cast spell. The\n110\t  automatic-firing portion is recorded in `untestable.json`.\n111\t- **Resolution of the copy / the copy being put into the graveyard.** The copy's\n112\t  resolution behavior is the underlying spell's, not this card's, and is covered\n113\t  by engine tests / the underlying spell's own contract.\n114\t- **Casualty paid with the dragon itself as the sacrifice.** Legal but a\n115\t  redundant variation of the generic power>=1 sacrifice already covered.\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c2fbc8b-9529-4254-888d-bbf9dd1c7145","uuid":"60379afd-5099-41c6-ae03-81002a9cd248","timestamp":"2026-06-02T01:38:55.706Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_226/test-rationale.md","content":"# Test rationale — sos_226 Silverquill, the Disputant\n\nCard: `{2}{W}{B}` Legendary Creature — Elder Dragon, 4/4.\nOracle: \"Flying, vigilance. Each instant and sorcery spell you cast has\ncasualty 1. (As you cast that spell, you may sacrifice a creature with power 1\nor greater. When you do, copy the spell and you may choose new targets for the\ncopy.)\"\n\nTest file: `/workspace/cards/sos/sos_226/tests.py` (27 tests).\n\n## Assumed API contract (so the Implementer can align)\n\nThe engine has **no native casualty mechanic**, and `engine.casting.cast_spell`\ndoes **not** fire any `SpellCastTriggeredEvent`. Following the established\nproject convention for granted abilities lacking native engine support\n(SOS 201's miracle granting via `grants_miracle_to` / `grant_miracle`, and the\n`copy_spell` surface used by FDN 248), the tests assume the card exposes:\n\n- `SilverquillTheDisputant` subclasses `engine.card.Creature` with:\n  - `name == \"Silverquill, the Disputant\"`, `mana_cost == {2}{W}{B}`,\n    `base_power == 4`, `base_toughness == 4`,\n    `supertypes` ⊇ `{LEGENDARY}`, `subtypes` ⊇ `{\"Elder\", \"Dragon\"}`,\n    `keywords` ⊇ `FLYING | VIGILANCE`, and `self.colors == [\"W\", \"B\"]`\n    (KEY_DECISIONS sos_13 explicit-colors convention).\n- `CASUALTY_AMOUNT` class constant equal to `1` (the granted casualty value).\n- `grants_casualty_to(spell) -> int | None` — returns `1` for an\n  instant/sorcery, `None` otherwise (mirrors `grants_miracle_to`).\n- `offer_casualty(game, stack_obj) -> bool` — the casualty offer. Given a spell\n  already on the stack as a `StackObject` (with `is_spell=True`), it:\n  1. returns `False` (no-op, no prompt) if the spell is not controlled by the\n     dragon's controller, or is not an instant/sorcery, or the controller has\n     no creature with `power >= 1` to sacrifice;\n  2. otherwise asks `choose_yes_no` to pay casualty; on decline returns `False`;\n  3. on accept asks `choose_card` to pick a power>=1 creature to sacrifice\n     (the legal options must exclude power-0 creatures), sacrifices it\n     (battlefield → graveyard), then asks `choose_yes_no` whether to choose new\n     targets for the copy; if yes, `choose_target` provides the new target;\n  4. pushes a `copy_spell(...)` of the spell onto the stack (a distinct object\n     from the original, `is_spell=True`, controlled by the dragon's controller,\n     with either the new targets or the original targets) and returns `True`.\n\nDecision order consumed from the player script in the accept path:\n`choose_yes_no (pay)` → `choose_card (sacrifice)` → `choose_yes_no (new targets?)`\n→ `choose_target (new target, only if yes)`. The Implementer may reorder the\n\"new targets\" question relative to the copy, but the tests script exactly one\nyes/no for it after the sacrifice. This contract was validated end-to-end with a\nthrowaway reference implementation: all 27 tests pass against it, then fail\nagainst the stub (TDD red phase).\n\n## Test groups\n\n### TestSilverquillProperties (7)\nStatic characteristics: is a `Creature`, name, mana cost `{2}{W}{B}`, 4/4,\nLEGENDARY supertype, Elder Dragon subtypes, and `self.colors == {W, B}`.\n\n### TestSilverquillKeywords (6)\n- `test_has_flying_and_vigilance` — both keyword flags present.\n- Flying combat consequences through the real `_can_block`: a ground creature\n  cannot block; a flyer or a reach creature can. The flyer/reach tests guard on\n  `Keyword.FLYING in attacker.keywords` so they only pass when Silverquill truly\n  flies (otherwise they would pass trivially against a non-flying stub).\n- `test_vigilance_does_not_tap_when_attacking` — drives the real\n  `declare_attackers_step`; the dragon is declared as an attacker and must\n  remain untapped (`is_attacking is True`, `is_tapped is False`), exercising the\n  engine's vigilance handling.\n\n### TestSilverquillCasualtyGrant (5)\nThe granted casualty value and eligibility capability: `CASUALTY_AMOUNT == 1`;\n`grants_casualty_to` returns `1` for an instant and a sorcery, and `None` for a\ncreature spell and a land.\n\n### TestSilverquillCasualtyOffer (9)\nThe core behavior, driving `offer_casualty` against a hand-built spell\n`StackObject`:\n- accept → sacrifice a power>=1 creature and push exactly one distinct copy of\n  the spell;\n- decline → no sacrifice, no copy;\n- no power>=1 creature available → no copy, **and no prompt** (verified via the\n  empty player script: a stray prompt raises `ScriptExhaustedError`);\n- power-0 creature is not a legal sacrifice option (a `choose_card` spy captures\n  the offered options and asserts the 0/4 wall is excluded while the 1/1 is\n  included);\n- choosing new targets points the copy at a different target than the original;\n- declining new targets keeps the copy's original targets;\n- the pushed copy is `is_spell=True` and controlled by the dragon's controller;\n- an opponent-controlled spell on the stack gets no offer (no prompt, no copy) —\n  \"spells *you* cast\";\n- a creature spell on the stack gets no offer — casualty is instant/sorcery only.\n\n### TestSilverquillCastingIntegration (1)\n`test_offer_casualty_on_really_cast_spell` — casts an instant through the REAL\n`engine.casting.cast_spell`, locates the genuine spell `StackObject` the engine\npushed, then drives `offer_casualty` against it and asserts the sacrifice +\nsingle copy. This proves the offer composes with a real cast (not just a\nhand-built StackObject).\n\n## Edge cases covered and why\n- Eligibility filter (power >= 1) — exercised both as a no-op (no eligible\n  creature) and as an option-filtering check (power-0 excluded).\n- \"You cast\" ownership gating (opponent's spell ignored).\n- Card-type gating (creature spell ignored; instant and sorcery both granted).\n- New-targets opt-in vs. opt-out for the copy.\n- Vigilance observed through real combat (no tap), not just the flag.\n\n## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered (out of scope / unblockable)\n- **Automatic wiring of the casualty offer into `cast_spell`.** The engine\n  fires no spell-cast event and the granted spell's own `on_cast` is not\n  Silverquill's, so there is no guaranteed automatic hook. The integration test\n  therefore drives `offer_casualty` explicitly against a really-cast spell. The\n  automatic-firing portion is recorded in `untestable.json`.\n- **Resolution of the copy / the copy being put into the graveyard.** The copy's\n  resolution behavior is the underlying spell's, not this card's, and is covered\n  by engine tests / the underlying spell's own contract.\n- **Casualty paid with the dragon itself as the sacrifice.** Legal but a\n  redundant variation of the generic power>=1 sacrifice already covered.\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
