{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBeufUv1X8ASvAZEwk9eBs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"requirement\": \"Each instant and sorcery spell you cast has casualty 1. (As you cast that spell, you may sacrifice a creature ...) — specifically the AUTOMATIC firing of the casualty offer during the spell's cast.\",\n4\t    \"reason\": \"engine.casting.cast_spell does not fire any SpellCastTriggeredEvent, and the granted spell's own on_cast hook belongs to the spell, not to Silverquill, so there is no guaranteed engine surface that automatically invokes Silverquill's casualty offer when its controller casts an instant/sorcery. The observable casualty offer (sacrifice + copy + optional new targets) IS fully tested via the documented offer_casualty(game, stack_obj) method against both a hand-built and a really-cast StackObject; only the automatic at-cast wiring is unverified.\",\n5\t    \"what_would_unblock\": \"An additive engine hook in engine.casting.cast_spell (analogous to the draw-time miracle hook added in engine.game.draw_card for SOS 201) that, after pushing a spell StackObject, queries permanents the caster controls for a casualty-granting capability (e.g. grants_casualty_to / offer_casualty) and invokes the offer. With that hook present, the integration test could assert that a plain engine_cast_spell of an instant — with no manual offer_casualty call — produces the sacrifice and the copy.\"\n6\t  }\n7\t]\n8\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SpkiziQuMtqYjFbVkMUryX","session_id":"7c2fbc8b-9529-4254-888d-bbf9dd1c7145","uuid":"ee515dca-ee5e-43e4-883b-a392716b9dc3","timestamp":"2026-06-02T01:43:25.275Z","subagent_type":"Tester","task_description":"Extend sos_226 tests: casualty auto-fire"}
